Erythromelalgia
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Intense burning extremity pain, Fiery erythema/localized warmth, Exacerbation by heat/relief with cold, Normal full blood count

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-012
Reason for Visit: Agonizing, fiery pain in the bilateral lower extremities causing severe acute distress and sleep deprivation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe, burning limb pain accompanied by fiery erythema, causing extreme distress and an absolute inability to bear weight or sleep. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out acute cellulitis, deep vein thrombosis, or severe peripheral neuropathy, and to provide immediate palliative pain management prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My feet are literally on fire, they are bright red and the burning is so agonizing I haven't slept in two days."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 48-hour severe flare of intensely painful, distinct burning sensation affecting the bilateral feet and distal lower legs. The pain is accompanied by pronounced, fiery erythema and severe localized heat. The symptoms were dramatically exacerbated tonight by sleeping under warm bedding. The patient notes profound, immediate but transient relief of the severe symptoms upon exposing the limbs to a cold tiled floor or extreme elevation. The pain is completely disrupting sleep and preventing weight-bearing.
Functional Status: Currently unable to walk due to severe tactile allodynia and thermal hyperalgesia on the soles of the feet.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound exhaustion and acute distress; living alone and unable to mobilize safely to the kitchen or bathroom.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, sitting with feet elevated completely uncovered, constantly fanning the lower extremities.
Physical Exam: Bilateral, highly symmetrical fiery erythema extending from the toes to the mid-calves. Noticeable severe warmth radiating from the affected extremities. Complete absence of palpable purpura, ulceration, or pitting edema. Peripheral pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) are bounding and completely intact, ruling out obvious peripheral arterial occlusion. No ascending lymphangitis. Documented history of an entirely normal full blood count effectively ruling out essential thrombocythaemia.
Environmental Observations: Bedroom window wide open; multiple ice packs melting on the bedroom floor.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Erythromelalgia - Status: Severe acute flare
Differentials:

Bilateral Cellulitis - Ruled out due to strict bilateral symmetry, immediate blanching on elevation, relief with cold, and completely normal body temperature.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out due to bilateral presentation, bounding pulses, and lack of unilateral calf swelling or pitting edema.

Peripheral Arterial Disease - Ruled out due to bounding distal pulses, localized extreme heat rather than cold, and intensely hyperemic appearance.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to agonizing, intractable burning limb pain and dramatic erythema causing severe functional impairment and profound distress. The clinical presentation mimicked acute, severe bilateral soft tissue infection or a profound vascular anomaly. An immediate physical examination was mandatory to clinically rule out acute cellulitis or deep vein thrombosis. Establishing the benign, functional nature of the extreme vasodilation allowed for immediate reassurance, avoidance of unnecessary broad-spectrum antibiotics, and the implementation of safe cooling strategies, fulfilling the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised absolute avoidance of direct ice application to prevent secondary frostbite. Prescribed topical lidocaine 5% ointment for symptomatic neuropathic relief and oral Gabapentin (titration commencing at 100mg nocte) for longer-term neuromodulation.
Positive Behavioral Change: Utilize a desktop fan directed at the uncovered feet rather than applying direct ice. Achieving sustained thermal relief via safe ambient cooling will positively reinforce the avoidance of direct ice, protecting the skin from irreversible cold injury while effectively managing the severe burning.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Small directional fan for bedside use.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 5 days to monitor Gabapentin titration and review autoimmune serology.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing distinct black or ulcerated skin lesions on the toes, unilateral extreme swelling, or high fevers with rigors.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis assumes previously normal blood work remains unchanged; secondary causes of erythromelalgia require ongoing specialist surveillance.
Clinical Course: This is a rare, episodic microvascular and neuropathic condition. Flares are intensely painful and triggered by mild heat, but do not inherently cause structural tissue damage if safe cooling is maintained.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if any toes turn blue or black, if the skin breaks down into open sores, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The tiny blood vessels in your feet are inappropriately dilating and dumping heat, irritating the local nerves. Ice causes an intense rebound flare when removed and risks frostbite; ambient air cooling is the only safe thermal management.
Actionable Steps: Sleep with your feet completely uncovered and elevated on a pillow, with a fan blowing directly across them. This continuous, safe convection cooling will steadily decrease the local temperature without triggering a rebound flare, allowing you to finally sleep.

SMS INFORMATION

